>From monst.c:

 * Guidelines for color assignment:
 *
 * * Use the same color for all `growth stages' of a monster (ex.
 *   little dog/big dog, baby naga/full-grown naga.
 *
 * * Use colors given in names wherever possible. If the class has `real'
 *   members with strong color associations, use those.
 *
 * * Favor `cool' colors for cold-resistent monsters, `warm' ones for
 *   fire-resistent ones.
 *
 * * Try to reserve purple (magenta) for powerful `ruler' monsters (queen
 *   bee, kobold lord, &c.).
 *
 * * Subject to all these constraints, try to use color to make as many
 *   distinctions as the / command (that is, within a monster letter
 *   distinct names should map to distinct colors).
 *
 * The aim in assigning colors is to be consistent enough so a player can
 * become `intuitive' about them, deducing some or all of these rules
 * unconsciously. Use your common sense.

In my opinion, this game doesn't always follow these rules consistently, 
so I got bored, sat down and made a spreadsheet of the use of monster 
colors in the game. It looks to me like there are a few problems with 
the current colors:

* Lack of color consistency within related monsters (for example, ettins 
are brown, yet ettin zombies/mummies are blue; werewolves are brown in 
animal form, yet orange in human form)

* Lack of color distinctions within a given letter (there are six kinds 
of brown "d" and eight kinds of non-unique red "&", for instance)

* Overuse of certain colors (especially brown and red) and underuse of 
others (orange, bright blue, etc.)

* This one is more subjective: the color magenta is used for so many 
"rulers" of dubious stature (kobold lords?) that it loses its 
significance as a marker of the truly dangerous.

I recognize that changing the existing colors offers potential for 
confusion and possibly fatal mistakes while adapting to the new colors, 
but I think that the changes would be well worthwhile once adapted to. I 
plan to make this into a patch, but I thought I'd ask for opinions on my 
selected colors before I do so.  So, without further ado, here's the 
